    - target: {fileID: 6163876341813508865, guid: 32cf0b5af8e50674fa364e0526edb2dc, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Text
      value: The Largemouth Bass (Micropterus salmoides) is one of the most important
        freshwater gamefish in the United States. A member of the sunfish family
        and a species of black bass native to North america. Able to live for up
        to 16 years and distinguished by their longer upper jaw, which, when closed,
        extends beyond the eye. They are the largest of the black basses, with the
        largest recorded overall length reaching 29.5 inches. Residing in the water
        of the Everglades their diet consists mainly of smaller fish, snails, crawfish,
        frogs, snakes, salamanders, bats, and even small water birds, mammals, and
        baby alligators. As one of the top predators in the waters they play an important
        job at maintaining the food webs balance. Without bass, the minnow population
        would soar out of control, and there would be overconsumption of zooplankton.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
